==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|galvanise||UK}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|galvaniser}}, from {{m|fr|galvanisme}}, {{named-after|en|nat=Italian|occ=physiologist|wplink=Luigi Galvani|Luigi Aloisio Galvani|born=1737|died=1798|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɡælvənaɪ̯z/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-galvanise.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# {{lb|en|chemistry}} To coat with a thin layer of [[metal]] by [[electrochemical]] means.
#: {{syn|en|electroplate|zinc|q2=rare}}
# To [[coat]] with rust-resistant [[zinc]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''galvanize''' steel}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[shock]] or [[stimulate]] into sudden activity, as if by [[electric shock]].
#: {{syn|en|animate|startle|urge}}
#: {{ux|en|The girl’s picture helped '''galvanize''' public opinion against the administration’s policy.}}
#: {{ux|en|Republicans are hoping a proposed gas-tax repeal will '''galvanize''' their voters.}}
#*{{quote-video game|en||developer={{w|BioWare}}|title={{w|Mass Effect: Andromeda}}|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2017|platform=PC|scene=Angara: The Resistance Codex entry|oclc=1261299044|text=The arrival of the kett stunned and then divided the angara. As described by Jaal, infighting was used as a means of conquest by the kett, but the presence of a common enemy eventually '''galvanized''' the angara to work together. The various resistance groups merged into the organized Angaran Resistance roughly five years ago under Evfra de Tershaav's command, supported by prominent families and determined to stop their kett oppressors.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[electrify]].
#* '''1835''', {{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay}}, ''History'' (essay in the ''Edinburgh Review'')
#*: The agitations resembled the grinnings and writhings of a '''galvanized''' corpse, not the struggles of an athletic man.
# {{lb|en|historical|US}} To switch sides between [[Union]] and [[Confederate]] in the [[American Civil War]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title={{w|Confederates in the Attic}}|author={{w|Tony Horwitz}}| year=1998| edition=1st Vintage Departures| publisher=Vintage Books| isbn=0679439781| page=10| passage=Reenactors called this “'''galvanizing''',” the Civil War term for soldiers who switched sides during the conflict.}}

